data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The checksum verification, implemented as an inner loop, calculates `s1` and `s2` using a specific formula. The computed checksum values (`s1`, `s2`) are then compared with the values extracted from the log at the expected positions (`log[endPayload]`, `log[endPayload + 1]`). This comparison confirms the integrity of the data within the frame.\n\nThe correctness of the checksum verification is confirmed by verifying multiple examples in the test cases. Frame type and size validations ensure data structure integrity. Incorrect format or checksum mismatch triggers the `WalException` with appropriate error code.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Phase 1 iterates, validating frame structure and checksums, while maintaining an \"intact\" records list. Checksum verification confirms data integrity. Invalid frames trigger exceptions. Frame type and size validations ensure structure integrity, specifically checksum mismatches.\n\nPhase 2 replays each \"intact\" record. It checks all rules R13 through R23d, focusing on payload layout for each type, like BEGIN, COMMIT, ABORT, PUT, DELETE, SAVEPOINT, ROLLBACK, and CHECKPOINT. Each type has specific payload length and content validation rules to confirm data consistency.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R13 through R23d now focus on payload rules for each record type within the replay phase. For type 8, `n` and `c` are checked for frame layout. For type 9 (READ), the key length (`k`) and flags (`f`) within the payload are validated with the size `n` relative to length `k`. An exception is thrown for invalid formats.\n\nThe transaction ID validation (R14) now specifies rules for all types. If the record type isn't 8, the transaction ID shouldn't be zero. For type 8, checkpoint records require checking each transaction ID within the checkpoint list to ensure it is also not zero.\n\nKey validation (R15) concerns types PUT, DELETE, and READ: key lengths, bounds (1 to 32), and byte ranges. If the key falls outside of the bounds, a `WalException` with the appropriate error code will be thrown.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"- For PUT and READ (with `f == 1`) records, the value's format is validated to ensure printable ASCII characters only. Any invalid character triggers a `BAD_VALUE` exception.\n- SAVEPOINT and ROLLBACK records have their names validated for the same printable ASCII character range.\n- The field checks' order (R13-R16a) and transaction ID checks are now verified against the spec.\n- Now beginning transaction ID checks are being implemented.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

