data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict condition analysis for the COMMIT action centers on overlapping writes and reads between the committing transaction (`U`) and the current transaction (`T`).  The written keys for transaction `U` are all the keys from its `PUT` and `DELETE` operations, considered at the time `U` commits, *after* any rollbacks.  The read set for transaction `T` consists of the keys from all its `READ` records processed before its own commit.  The `READ_MISMATCH` rule implies that all successfully processed `READ` records are present in `T`'s `readSet`, independent of `f`. The analysis now focuses on efficiently identifying and comparing these key sets.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R22 COMMIT and R23 ABORT logic is finalized.  COMMIT now checks for key conflicts; if detected, the transaction's writes are discarded, and the transaction is added to aborted. If no conflicts are found, the transaction's writes are applied and the transaction is committed. Both actions remove the transaction from open transactions. R23a savepoint behavior is validated.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `ROLLBACK` action's logic is finalized based on R23b. A savepoint `s` with position `n` is identified. The write list retains the first `n` writes and discards subsequent writes, effectively \"rolling back\" the transaction to the savepoint. Any savepoints set at a larger offset than the one that set `s` are removed. The state map is *not* modified, just the write list.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `ROLLBACK` action's logic is finalized. A savepoint `s` with position `n` is identified; the write list retains the first `n` writes, discarding subsequent writes. Savepoints with the *same position* but set at a *larger offset* than `s` are removed, while savepoint `s` itself is retained. The transaction remains open. State and read sets are unchanged.\n\nConsider the implications of removing a savepoint set at a larger offset. Tracing an example ensures the write list has at least `n` writes after rollback. After rolling back to savepoint \"b\" (offset 40, pos 2), savepoint \"a\" (offset 50, pos 2) should be removed, and \"b\" should stay, and the write list size is truncated to the second write.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"`setOffset > 50`, so \"b\" (pos=2, setOffset=40) stays. A rollback to \"b\" guarantees writes still contain 2 or more writes. A savepoint's position never exceeds the number of writes at the time it was set; a subsequent rollback cannot leave a savepoint with a position beyond the write list size. This invariant allows the final logic to safely remove the savepoint with a larger `setOffset`, ensuring the write list is truncated to the rollback position. The implementation for R23b is therefore complete.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

