data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R7 and R8 apply even if the record doesn't fit in the log. Verifying the rule order is crucial. `L - p` equals 3 bytes and the check should start from the byte at `p`. If it is `00 00 04`, R7 applies and throws `UNKNOWN_TYPE`. If it is `01 04 01`, R8 applies, and throws `RECORD_TOO_LARGE`. Confirmed.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial concern focuses on R9 and R10, specifically edge cases with torn tails and checksum mismatches where the record ends close to the log end. The current implementation appears to handle these conditions correctly based on the sequence of checks. The Fletcher-16 checksum calculation's modulo 255 behavior is being re-evaluated for potential edge case handling. The calculation should produce the proper values because of the way in which the value is calculated, which should still return a checksum within the expected range.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R7 and R8 apply regardless of frame size, and the order of operations matters to avoid index-out-of-bounds errors. R9 and R10 handle checksums. Investigating a checksum with a byte value of 255 (0xFF). Since s1 and s2 are always mod 255, any checksum byte of 255 should be flagged as an invalid checksum. The formula directly implements the pseudocode. Now, let's look at `PUT` records where the size `N` is small or large.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

