data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Initial suspicion was a cache miss, but traces show database latency is the actual bottleneck. Binary search within the versioned history, is mathematically identical to a snapshot. Version `0` reads are consistent with the initial `state`. After committing changes in `T2` to `a`, version `1` stores the updated value. `T1` will remain unaffected and unaffected by the updates in `T2`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The versioned history implementation provides a way to trace history. The binary search on history maintains a consistent read snapshot. Deleted keys are handled by storing `null` values with respective versions, ensuring proper retrieval across all versions.\n\nMemory overhead for a log with a maximum of 8 writes and a maximum of 1000 commits is addressed. BEGIN operations are O(1) in both time and space; COMMIT operations scale O(writes) due to the append of `VersionedValue` entries; READ scales with O(writes + log(commits)) because the history has the most recent version, where writes are capped at 8 and binary searches are capped at log(1000) ~10. Conflict detection in COMMIT scales linearly to transaction log and is bounded at O(8) writes.\n\nNow examining the conflict detection for COMMIT, focusing on the potential read/write conflicts. The conflict condition hinges on the order of transaction BEGIN and COMMIT records. The definition's precision ensures correct conflict detection across all scenarios, which indicates that conflict detection scales with writes.\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The time complexity of conflict detection in R22 is being analyzed, particularly focusing on optimization. The goal is to reduce it from a potentially quadratic relationship to a complexity dependent only on the size of the current transaction's read set. The idea is to keep track of the largest commit offset for each written key across all previously committed transactions.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict check boils down to determining, for each key read by the current transaction (`T`), whether any *previous* committed transaction (`U`) wrote to that key *after* `T`'s beginning. The goal now is to optimize this check. To optimize this, keeping track of the largest commit offset for each key across all committed transactions `U` will allow a straightforward comparison to `T`'s begin offset, for each key in `T`'s read set. This will allow the optimization, reducing the search to finding a key-specific maximum.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

