data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict check centers on efficiently determining if any committed transaction wrote a key read by the current transaction after it began. If the state map does not change, then lastCommittedWriteOffset must update. Updating `lastCommittedWriteOffset` with the *commit offset* for every written key in a committing transaction guarantees the largest write offset for any key is tracked, even for deleted or overwritten keys. This allows a direct comparison against the current transaction's begin offset to determine conflict. This simplifies the conflict detection to checking each key within a read set against the keyed offset.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The conflict check iterates through the current transaction's read set, checking if any committed transaction has written the read key *after* the current transaction began. The largest write offset is tracked, allowing direct comparison with the current transaction's begin offset, simplifying conflict detection. The entire `recover` method's complexity is being assessed: Phase 1 is a linear pass O(L) on the log, and Phase 2 involves linear processing of intact records, with O(1) or O(log(commits)) operations, resulting in a runtime well within limits and low memory usage. The `VersionedValue` and `lastCommittedWriteOffset` data structures and update logic during transaction commits are being thoroughly re-evaluated.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The `stateHistory` now efficiently tracks all key versions.  Each transaction's committed writes append to this history; conflicts trigger discarding writes, and the versioning ensures consistent read snapshots. The logic ensures each `currentVersion` has only one entry per key, even with multiple writes within a single commit.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"The binary search in `lookupSnapshot` now clearly distinguishes between an absent key (returns `null` from `stateHistory.get(key)`) and a present key with an empty string value (returns a `VersionedValue` with a `value` of `\"\"`). A key can have an empty string value or be completely removed via `DELETE`. The `value` field within the `VersionedValue` record explicitly handles a distinction between a key being present or absent.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

data: {"id":"chatcmpl-9fb03431-7c52-4cc2-87d4-1d9b21530100","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1795544584,"model":"gemini-4-carbon","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{"reasoning_content":"R22 is verified by tracking the `lastCommittedWriteOffset` for each key. During a `COMMIT`, the map updates, tracking the largest commit offset. The check now verifies against the `T`'s `beginOffset`. The `stateHistory` maintains all key versions. The algorithm efficiently tracks versions using binary search within the transaction and uses `null` to denote deletion, distinct from an empty string value, as an effective bijection. The write list lookup correctly handles DELETE operations by setting the value to `null`.\n\n\n"},"finish_reason":null}],"usage":null}

